2026-09-27T10:28:42.0454288Z Disclaimer: there's no guarantee of timely updates in case of security problems or other issues. If you want to use them in a security-or-otherwise-critical environment (say, on a production server), you do so at your own risk.
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0455012Z 
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0455083Z Update Note
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0455244Z ===========
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0455476Z Please use this repository instead of ppa:fkrull/deadsnakes.
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0455708Z 
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0455782Z Reporting Issues
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0455961Z ================
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0456060Z 
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0456191Z Issues can be reported in the master issue tracker at:
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0456486Z https://github.com/deadsnakes/issues/issues
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0456663Z 
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0456753Z Supported Ubuntu and Python Versions
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0456976Z ====================================
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0457119Z 
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0457227Z - Ubuntu 22.04 (jammy) Python3.7+ (NOT Python3.10)
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0457534Z - Ubuntu 24.04 (noble) Python3.7+ (NOT Python3.12)
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0457824Z - Ubuntu 26.04 (resolute) Python3.7+ (NOT Python3.14)
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0458206Z - Note: Python 3.10 (jammy), Python3.12 (noble), Python3.14 (resolute) are not provided by deadsnakes as upstream ubuntu provides those packages.
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0458473Z 
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0458533Z Why some packages aren't built:
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0458763Z - Note: for 22.04+, older python versions requre libssl<3 so they are not currently built
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0459043Z - If you need these, reach out to asottile to set up a private ppa
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0459190Z 
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0459348Z The packages may also work on other versions of Ubuntu or Debian, but that is not tested or supported.
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0459553Z 
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0459600Z Packages
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0459701Z ========
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0459757Z 
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0460386Z The packages provided here are loosely based on the debian upstream packages with some modifications to make them more usable as non-default pythons and on ubuntu.  As such, the packages follow debian's patterns and often do not include a full python distribution with just `apt install python#.#`.  Here is a list of packages that may be useful along with the default install:
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0461059Z 
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0461197Z - `python#.#-dev`: includes development headers for building C extensions
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0461607Z - `python#.#-venv`: provides the standard library `venv` module
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0462170Z - `python#.#-distutils`: provides the standard library `distutils` module
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0462479Z - `python#.#-lib2to3`: provides the `2to3-#.#` utility as well as the standard library `lib2to3` module
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0462769Z - `python#.#-gdbm`: provides the standard library `dbm.gnu` module
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0462997Z - `python#.#-tk`: provides the standard library `tkinter` module
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0517598Z 
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0517717Z Third-Party Python Modules
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0517923Z ==========================
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0518043Z 
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0519010Z Python modules in the official Ubuntu repositories are packaged to work with the Python interpreters from the official repositories. Accordingly, they generally won't work with the Python interpreters from this PPA. As an exception, pure-Python modules for Python 3 will work, but any compiled extension modules won't.
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0520020Z 
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0520544Z To install 3rd-party Python modules, you should use the common Python packaging tools.  For an introduction into the Python packaging ecosystem and its tools, refer to the Python Packaging User Guide:
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0521228Z https://packaging.python.org/installing/
2026-09-27T10:28:42.0521402Z 
